*Where the answers failed.* The loop answered all 40 meaning questions and Base answered 36. The loop returned “The shelf” both when the obstacle was “too tall” (the pot) and when it was “too high” (the shelf). In the water example both systems chose the cup when the sentence changed from “until it was empty” to “until it was full.” Among the 16 file messages, the first executed keyed lookup used the filename column on only two messages per model. The loop selected bytes eight times and the digest column four times, with no executed lookup twice. Base selected bytes seven times and the digest column three times. Four messages had no executed lookup. Only one file answer per system was both correct and grounded. Base also produced a second correct quantity outside the registered operational endpoint.

*Decision.* Base alone solved one complete meaning pair. Across all 28 units there were zero recurrent wins and one loss (exact two-sided paired $p=1$). The loop met the wall, peak and throughput gates. It missed the individual, paired reasoning, tool, pooled and first-token gates. Histories diverged after the first user request in each file conversation. A higher aggregate opening rate can thus coexist with more total time to first token.

*Independent mechanism check.* On a separate 25-token natural prompt, switching the second pass's residual contribution from 0.175 to zero changed the full 248,320-way final-position logits by RMS 0.3284 and maximum 1.8359. Restoring 0.175 reproduced every active logit exactly. The most likely token stayed the same. The two passes referenced identical objects for all seven projections and independent KV tensors at offsets 25 and 25. This fixed-prefix intervention establishes an effect on model computation; the accuracy evidence comes from the free requests above.
